Polymorphism -> Same name, different behaviors,
    o Variable overriding.
    o Method overriding (child redefines parent method),
    o Method overloading (using default arguments).

    Polymorphism means one thing can behave in many different ways.
    It is like one person playing different roles in different situations.
    For example, the same person can be a father at home, an employee at office, and a friend outside.
    Even though the person is the same, the behavior changes based on the situation.
    In programming, this means one function or action can work differently depending on the input or context.

    📘 Polymorphism — Key Fundamentals 🤔
        🔹 One interface (name) → Multiple behaviors
        🔹 Same method/function works differently based on context
        🔹 Achieved mainly through Overriding and Overloading
        🔹 Improves flexibility and extensibility of code
        🔹 Supports dynamic behavior at runtime

    💎 One-line:
        🎭 One action → Many forms
        ✅ Advantages of Polymorphism 😄
        ✔️ Code reusability
        ✔️ Flexible design (easy to extend)
        ✔️ Cleaner and more readable code
        ✔️ Reduces conditional logic (if-else chains)
        ✔️ Supports plug-and-play behavior

        👉 Same interface, different implementations 👍

    ⚠️ Limitations of Polymorphism 🤔

        🔴 1) Harder to understand for beginners
        👉 Behavior depends on object type

        🔴 2) Debugging can be difficult
        👉 Method execution not obvious

        🔴 3) Runtime overhead
        👉 Dynamic method resolution

        🔴 4) Overuse leads to complex design
        👉 Too many variations = confusion 😵

        🔴 5) Python overloading support limited
        👉 Not like Java/C++

    🛠️ Solutions / Best Practices 😎
        ✔️ Use clear naming conventions
        ✔️ Apply only where flexibility is needed
        ✔️ Keep hierarchy simple
        ✔️ Use documentation & type hints
        ✔️ Prefer composition if behavior becomes too complex

    🏆 Real-Life Analogy 💡

        📱 Mobile phone:
        ✔️ Same “Call” button
        ✔️ Calls different people based on number

        Same action → different result

📘 Overriding vs Overloading — Comparison 🤔:-
        🔑 Feature	            🔄 Method Overriding	            ➕ Method Overloading
        Meaning	Child class     changes parent method behavior	    Same method name with different inputs
        Where it happens	    Between Parent & Child classes	    Usually within the same class
        Purpose	                Modify inherited behavior	        Provide multiple ways to perform same action
        Parameters	            Must be SAME	                    Must be DIFFERENT
        Inheritance needed	    ✅ Yes	                            ❌ Not required
        Runtime / Compile	    Runtime polymorphism	            Compile-time concept (Python simulates)
        Python support	        ✅ Fully supported	                ⚠️ Limited support
        Example idea	        Parent method replaced in child	    Same method handles different arguments

        🧠 Two Independent Concepts
        1️⃣ MRO decides WHO is next
        2️⃣ super() decides WHETHER to go next
        ⭐ super() searches forward in MRO until method found 🤔
        🌟 super().method() = call that method in next suitable class
        “super() only tells go or not and what method to execute”

        ✔️ super() alone → proxy only
        ✔️ super().method() → executes method
        ✔️ Uses next class in MRO
        ✔️ Skips classes without that method
        ✔️ Stops at first match

